WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:09.340
By the 1950s the lock and key picture decorated every textbook, and Daniel Koshland

2
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.780
knew it was incomplete because of water.

3
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:17.353
If an active site sat permanently open and waiting, water, smaller than any

4
00:00:17.353 --> 00:00:20.453
substrate, should slip in constantly and trigger chaos.

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:22.233
It doesn't.

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:27.443
So something must change when the real substrate arrives: the site closes around it,

7
00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:32.063
and the complementarity that matters is reached not at the resting substrate but at

8
00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:33.823
the strained transition state.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:38.243
Binding induces the fit, and water is too small to force it.

10
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:43.807
The idea cut so hard against the dogma that the standard journals turned it away;

11
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:50.447
one reviewer's verdict became famous ("What is new is not true, and what is true is

12
00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:51.447
not new").

13
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.880
Koshland slipped the paper into PNAS in 1958 through a loophole, and X-ray

14
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.640
crystallography later proved him right: enzymes flex, shift, and clamp down.

15
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.540
He had moved biochemistry from a science of static shapes to one of moving machines.
